turns.content:
Oh, that’s such a painful mix of joy and pressure. You love Shreya, you’ve imagined this moment for years, and you also want to honour Ramzan and your own limits. Both feelings can be true—you can be thrilled for her and genuinely scared of how exhausting the night may be.

You don’t have to choose between “show up exactly as planned” and “let her down.” Maybe talk to her soon and be honest: *“I absolutely want to do the opening dance with you. I’m fasting, though, so I need us to plan it in a way that’s sustainable for me—shorter choreography, rehearsals earlier, a place to pray, and no pressure to stay through every part of the night.”*

Depending on your practice and what feels right to you, you could also:
- rehearse after iftar rather than pushing through fasting hours;
- keep the opening dance simple and beautiful;
- arrange a quiet room and time for prayer;
- break your fast privately or with a small trusted group before the event;
- make sure someone else can co-direct the logistics so you aren’t carrying the whole sangeet;
- leave after the dance if you need to, without treating that as failure.

And please don’t wait until you’re running on fumes to set the boundary. Shreya may be heartbroken at the thought of changing anything, but she’ll probably much rather have a supported, present version of you than watch you force yourself through the night. You’re her best friend—not the entire production crew, and not obligated to prove your love by exhausting yourself.
